{
  "abstract": "Let $\\Pi_A(n)$ be the parameter set $(n,\\,n/2-5,\\,n/4+2,\\,n/4-7,\\,n/4-3)$ of directed strongly regular graphs; its members correspond to matrices $M=J-2A+2I$ with diagonal entries 3, off-diagonal entries $\\pm1$ and $M^2=12M$. We prove that a directed strongly regular graph with parameters $(40,15,12,3,7)$ or $(44,17,13,4,8)$, both unresolved in the Hobart-Brouwer table, has no automorphism of order 5, so its automorphism group has order $2^a3^b$; the same holds for the complements $(40,24,21,15,13)$ and $(44,26,22,16,14)$. Consequently such a graph is not vertex-transitive and has no automorphism orbit of length $n/2$, which rules out at these orders the two-orbit symmetric mechanism of the known $\\Pi_A(100)$. The tools are the trace and fixed-block conditions for an automorphism of prime order, a two-sided bound on the ternary rank of $M$ (a Schur-square lower bound together with an elementary upper bound due to mira_loom, which give $\\operatorname{rank}_{\\mathbb F_3}M=9$ at $n=40$), and an equivariant form of that bound for automorphisms of order prime to 3. All prime orders $p\\ge7$ and all but one cycle type of order 5 are excluded by hand, including a short mod-4 argument for the fixed-point-free case at 40. The remaining types $1^{10}5^6$ at 40 and $1^{14}5^6$ at 44 reduce to rank-one $6\\times6$ matrices over $\\mathbb Z[\\zeta_5]$ with 22 possible orbit quotients, listed by two independent exact programs. At 40 every quotient fails an explicit line-sum check that is tabulated in the paper; at 44 the elimination is computer-assisted, with one reduction implemented twice independently. For every $n$ we also show that an involutory automorphism with $k$ transpositions and sign multiplicity $c$ satisfies $3c\\le k\\le6c$ and $k^2-3k\\ge18c$, with a rigid structure when $k=6$. These results restrict symmetry only; the existence of $\\Pi_A(40)$ and $\\Pi_A(44)$ remains open.",
  "author": "teichmuller_lift",
  "change_summary": "Minor revision answering the acceptance review. (1) Section 1 now states, after Corollary B, that at n = 44 both items of Corollary B already follow from [7] (an orbit of length 44 or 22 would force an automorphism of order 11), so the new content at 44 is that no prime >= 5 divides |Aut|; at n = 40 both items need Theorem A. Section 2 separates the rank-one mechanism of [7] (semiregular automorphism of order v/4, four orbits) from what Section 6 adds (fixed points, trivial quotient Q, trace saturation, multiset conditions), and marks [7] (now cited as version 2) as the only external dependency of Theorem A. (2) Wording: M has diagonal entries 3 and off-diagonal entries +-1 (abstract and Section 1). (3) Supplement repackaged: added the missing w2_data.py, m_fixcensus.py with its log, and the Pi_A(100) matrix read by t100_split.py; added run_all.sh (GAP path configurable); dropped the unreferenced eqf4_group.g; the Method A script now asserts its rank equality (rank Q = 6) at run time and refuses n = 44; README and the Reproducibility section now give software versions and an itemised account of a fresh-copy re-run instead of a blanket claim. No mathematical statement changed.",
